WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.020
Hello everybody, my name is Markiplier

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:03.880
and welcome to The Uncle Who Works for Nintendo.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.660
Now this horror game is going to be WAY different

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.120
than anything that you've seen on this channel before

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:10.680
because this one is

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:11.260
[slight dramatic pause]

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.840
an INTERACTIVE horror game.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.700
Well, I guess most horror games are interactive

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.740
but this one is actually a "choose your own adventure" style narrative-driven horror game

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.080
and if that may seem weird...

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.140
it might be, I don't know how it's gonna go

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.780
but it says there are tons of jump scares (have spare underwear handy)

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:28.840
which could be good or bad.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.960
"You are 11 years old. What is your best friend's name?"

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:33.240
[classic mark obama noise] Agh,

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:34.580
none of these people!

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:35.720
uhhhh

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.760
I don't know if this matters

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.080
but I'm gonna go with Daaaaaave.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.880
"Your best friend Dave has invited you to a sleepover at his house this weekend!"

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:43.760
YIPPEE!

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.820
"You've been friends since first grade, so asking your mom is basically only a formality."

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.800
"You've never confirmed this, but you suspect the babysitter may charge extra when there are two kids.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.940
You sometimes feel like staying somewhere else is the only way you can help out."

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:56.400
Oh!

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.160
Oh, that's sweet and sad!

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.840
Awwww he's trying to help out your mom

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:01.960
oh cool

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:07.520
"On Friday night you're home for only a few hours; long enough to pack, get in a fight with your younger sister, pack some more, and watch some TV.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.240
At six sharp you're standing on the sidewalk outside Dave's house while your mom idles in her car nearby.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.360
She leans out the window to you."

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.080
"'You behave yourself, okay?' she says, as always.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.960
'I'll be at work, but if anything happens, you call me.'"

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.340
[loud groan] Okay, mom! Go-!

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.000
Awe - mom works the night shift - awe.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.100
"'I'll pick you up tomorrow at three,' she says, again as usual.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.100
But then she pauses, looking up at the sky,

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.980
which has been overcast throughout the day.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.540
'If you play outside,' she adds, 'be careful. It's probably gonna rain.'"

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.880
[mockingly] Well I'm not gonna give my mom a kiss right in front of Dave!

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.100
I can't embarrass myself like that - I'm a young boy!

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.100
"After you part your mom drives down the street, disappearing around the corner.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.400
You turn back to Dave's house, the lights inside are glowing warmly.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.200
You can see Dave waving at you from his bedroom on the second floor."

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.580
He would've seen!

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:55.740
He would've seen that and he would've made fun of me - I can't have none of that.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.880
Oh...? [dramatic pause]

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.680
"It is 6 p.m."

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.280
I haven't played a choose-your-own-adventure game like this -

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.420
I mean, I've played games like this before and the details are very important

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:12.320
in these types of games so you have to pay attention to the dialogue very closely.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.320
"Dave's mom meet you just inside.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.060
'Hello!' she says. 'Dinner will be ready in just a few minutes,

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.840
but you can drop stuff off in the den.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.640
You and Dave are camping out there tonight.'"

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.640
Hah, cool! Thanks Dave's mom!

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.080
Ah, you're the coolest - way cooler than my mom.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.960
"You drop your sleeping and overnight bags in the corner at the den and then pause to take a look around.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.740
Behind the couch is a grandfather clock ticking softly,

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.700
through a set of patio doors on the far side of the room you can see the sky

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.700
is just as gray as it was when your mom left.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.340
Framed pictures line the walls and over the dark fireplace hangs

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.440
a monstrous pair of antlers from a buck that Dave's dad shot years ago.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.160
And of course, there is the big screen tv."

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.400
"Sometimes it makes you uncomfortable how much nicer Dave's house is than yours."

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.060
Oh, man - I'm an ungrateful brat kid!

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.320
Awe, your moms trying! My mom's trying!

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:57.900
"Dave entered the room while you weren't paying attention,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.580
and he now stands in the doorway smiling expectantly.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.580
'Are you ready for dinner?' he asks."

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.300
Suuure. Heh, HEH - HECK YEAH!

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.300
I haven't had dinner in days!

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.300
"Dinner passes quickly,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.180
tonight's meal is spaghetti and meatballs, one of Dave's favorite meals,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.520
as his mother points out while piling a helping on your plate.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.400
Dave's father cracks a beer and jovially interrogates you

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.100
about how much trouble you and Dave are getting into at school."

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.960
"Wait, that wasn't' a beer at all, that's a glass of lemonade.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:24.280
Why would you even think it was a beer?

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.720
Dave's father doesn't drink alcohol. You're quite certain of that fact,

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:27.760
now that you've remembered it."

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.260
Well, whatever!

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:32.120
Dave's father s- OOOOOHHHHH

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.680
Ah! This is what I meant by paying attention to details

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.120
because THAT changed the story just a little bit

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:38.800
JUST A LITTLE BIT

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.080
I think that's, like, a tutorial introduction to

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.720
IF YOU PAY ATTENTION IT'S GOING TO DO DIFFERENT THINGS!

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.300
okay - "the grandfather clock chimes in the den

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.300
[chime of grandfather clock]

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.300
"'You go along now,' says Dave's mom,

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.900
smiling from her side at the table, 'we'll clean up in here.'

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.900
'Let's go get the TV ready,' says Dave.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.620
The two of you leave the dining room and head upstairs."

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.480
"Dave's room is immense.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.580
You stay in the den because the TV is larger there,

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.500
but there's a sizable one here,

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.680
flush with the wall opposite the full-size bed.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.180
'We'll take down the 64 first,' says Dave,

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.180
heading toward his TV and opening the entertainment center cabinet."

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.840
"It's his prerogative of course, he gets to choose what you play first, usually.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:18.220
But as Dave begins unhooking the cords of the N64 from the TV,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.260
you catch sight of other things he has in there."

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.600
"All the major stuff: an old SNES, a PlayStation, a Dreamcast...

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.340
But some other things, too, Things you don't really recognize.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.180
A large black box with green highlights, a smaller purple one,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.700
a strange white and yellow tower with what look like gloves

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.680
resting on hooks on either side,

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.680
a compact white cone..."

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.000
What are those?

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.140
WHAT ARE THOOOOO - okay, shut up.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.900
"Dave looks to the clutter in the cabinet.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:48.180
'Oh yeah, they're pretty cool. I can't show them to you though, they're still secret. I promised my uncle.'

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.180
Of course, you suddenly remember, his uncle."

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:51.700
[pause]

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:55.540
[mysteriously:] The Uncle Who Works for Nintendo

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:56.800
"In the corner the gran-"

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.220
[pause to listen to strange noises]

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.000
Oh! It's vidi- IT'S VIDJA GAMES!

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.000
It didn't sound good.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.000
"The grandfather clock is ticking;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.920
it's 7:00 p.m."

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.640
Framed pictures. "You walk around the perimeter of the den, inspecting the pictures idly.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:11.840
Most of them are family portraits from years past:

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.680
Dave cradled lovingly between his mother and father,

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.680
or any one of the three on their own.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.160
A happy, tidy family."

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.180
Look for pictures of Dave's UNCLE? Very important.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.940
"You don't find any. Not one.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.940
The only pictures here are of Dave and his parents.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:27.360
You don't know why but that makes you feel uneasy."

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.360
"Time passes." OH! I didn't -

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:30.820
Oh.

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:32.280
Oh! Cool, okay.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.680
Why do I need to think about Dave's uncle?

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:35.420
Why -

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:36.260
Why do I -

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:36.820
Why -

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.020
Why's that an option?!

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:39.880
I'm gonna go - that's just so weird.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.880
"It began with Mew."

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:42.580
Did it?

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.920
"You didn't believe him at first, when Dave came to school one day

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.760
and told you he had finally caught Mew.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.560
'Prove it,' you said. So he pulled out his Gameboy and showed you."

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.040
"There it was. Mew.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:52.480
The 151st Pokémon

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.480
available only to players at promotional events,

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.480
somehow unlocked on Dave's game.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.240
'It's really strong; it KO's every enemy in one hit.'"

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:01.240
"Dave demonstrated this claim at recess,

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.500
when you and some other friends linked Gameboys to do battle.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:04.800
You were the first one down.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.800
No one else got in a single hit on Dave's Mew.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.720
In a few days everyone had quit playing Pokémon at recess."

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.480
The allure had faded.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:14.720
You asked how he managed to get it. 'Oh, my uncle got a job at Nintendo,' said Dave.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.760
You were walking home together, past one of the construction crews.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.440
There had been a storm not too long ago. Trees were down all over town. Buildings had collapsed.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:24.060
You were standing at an intersection with Dave as a truck rumbled by,

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.060
loaded up with ragged tree trunks.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.500
What a bad storm.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.680
Dave still lived next door to you at the time.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.900
Wait, this couldn't have been too long ago...when did Dave move?

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:34.740
There was something nice about being neighbors."

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.340
Man, your memory is really foggy here.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:40.420
"'He also got me this new Gameboy,' pulling it out of his pocket.

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:44.040
You hadn't noticed earlier, but yes, Dave now had a sleek new Gameboy Color.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.200
Until today, he only had one of the old ones,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.200
a big gray brick like yours.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.160
'This one's a special edition, isn't it cool?' You agreed."

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:52.760
[nodding] Jealously.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.520
"You snap out of your reflections.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.320
The clock chimes." [grandfather clock chimes]

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.040
Is it eight? It's eight now. Or is it nine?

00:06:59.040 --> 00:06:59.940
It's eight, okay.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.720
"Remember your own Mew."

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:04.260
[shockingly] WHAT?! When did I have a Mew?!

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:05.660
When would I have a Mew?!

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.340
"You got your own Mew, eventually.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.420
Another friend had a GameShark, which you borrowed one day.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.360
You spent that entire night unlocking every Pokémon you couldn't obtain

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.780
in your copy of the game or hadn't traded in yet.

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:16.180
Including Mew.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.020
It didn't one-hit KO most enemies.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:21.480
It was incredibly weak, and you shamefully cheated the game further to make it strong enough.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.540
It even looked different from Dave's.

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.540
Your Mew was small, even cute,

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:28.060
standing there with its round, cheerful eyes.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.060
But when Dave's had wiped out everyone at school

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:31.900
it had looked completely different:

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:34.380
compact, snarling, fierce.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:35.440
You asked him why.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:38.040
'What?' he said. That had been here, in the den.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:39.320
'Oh, that thing?'

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:42.760
Dave had moved by then. But how long was that after the first storm, after he got Mew?

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.600
You can't remember.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:46.680
It had been a while since anyone talked about Pokémon.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:49.000
'Well, my uncle got me the special edition Mew, first of all,'

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.940
he said, smirking a little, but not looking away from the PlayStation game he was playing.

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:54.460
'That's why mine looked different.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:57.500
Second of all, mine can one-hit KO because it's the real Mew.'

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.120
You asked what he meant by that.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.560
'Just what I said. You cheated, and got a fake Mew.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.800
So of course there'd be problems. Glitches and junk.'

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.800
You felt your cheeks redden.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:08.380
'But not me, I got the real Mew. And only me!

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.380
My brother was SO jealous.'

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:11.840
Dave's brother...

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.760
What about him? Why does remembering that comment make you feel uneasy?

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.760
You snap out of your reflections."

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.760
I'm not sure what's going on here

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:22.500
But it looks like I'm out of things to remember and it's only 8:30.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:23.760
So I'm gonna talk to Dave!

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.100
Ask him if he thinks this storm will be as bad as the last one -

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.440
that might be important considering - oh, jeez -

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.940
considering I'm wondering if HE remembers the storm

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:34.780
considering memories seem to be a little, kerfuffled.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.580
"I don't like the sound of the wind. You remember that storm we had that tore down all the trees?"

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:40.580
"'I don't know what you're talking abou-'" I KNEW IT!

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:44.260
IIIIII KNEW IT! Things are a little ~kerfuckled~ here.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.260
"You don't remember? 'Remember what?'

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:49.800
I actually forgot until tonight. There was this huge storm a few weeks ago, I think...

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.500
'If it was important I'm sure I would have remembered it.'"

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:54.140
Oooh.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.140
Oh, no.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.020
He don't remember. Alright, how about games?

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:02.240
"You bring up the subject of the new Pokémon games through the rumors you heard on the internet

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.640
when you were supposed to be working in school.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:07.620
Dave meticulously debunks or confirms everything you have to say,

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:09.620
all based on, of course, what his uncle has told him.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.960
An hour passes. 
[grandfather clock chimes] 
You are in the den.

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:14.320
Dave's mother bustles into the room,

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.220
holding this large ceramic bowl filled with popcorn under her arm.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.440
'How are you kids doing?' she asks.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:22.200
'Good,' says Dave, his eyes not moving from the television.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:25.540
'I hope you're having fun. Here's some popcorn - extra butter!'

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.020
You're welcome!

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:29.960
"Almost immediately Dave is shoveling popcorn into his mouth.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.540
Meanwhile, his mother smiles first at him and then back at you.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.440
'There's sodas in the kitchen if you get thirsty,

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.080
and some pizza from the other night, if you get hungry.'"

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.260
Thank you! THANK YOU!

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:42.700
""Your father's gone to bed. I'll be there soon, myself,

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.580
I want you two to keep it quiet. Alright?'

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:45.580
'Yeeessss, mom.'

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:47.280
'Oh, and before I forget, your uncle called.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.280
He suddenly has some business here in town tomorrow,

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:52.300
but he's driving in early. He'll be here around midnight.'"

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:54.300
Oh, that doesn't sound good at all!

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:55.760
Oh, no!

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:59.460
"For the first time Dave stops playing his game, stops eating popcorn, and turns to look at his mother.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:01.000
'Okay,' he says.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.560
'I want you two to welcome him in.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:05.780
He'll be very tired and very hungry, so offer him something to eat before he goes to bed.'

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:08.820
'Okay, mom.' 'Goodnight, kids!' And she's gone."

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.460
Aaah

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:12.300
Ooo, bowl of popcorn

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:13.640
It's buttery and delicious!

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:14.920
Okay, what about this uncle?

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:17.760
Why does he come in for business but I thought he worked for Nintendo?

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.240
"'He does,' frowning but not looking away from the TV,

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:22.000
'He's really important there.'

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.860
Does Nintendo have a lot of business here?

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:27.220
'Why else would my uncle be coming?' as if you've asked the dumbest question in the world.

